+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Register caching (--reg-cache)
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Lifted code reads and writes every SPU register as ctx->gpr[N]. Local-store
+# stores go through byte pointers into the same context (and the TUs build with
+# -fno-strict-aliasing), so after EVERY stqd the compiler must assume any
+# register may have changed: each instruction reloaded its operands from
+# memory and stored its result, the loop-invariant LS gates and ctx->ls were
+# reloaded per access, and nothing stayed in a host register across a store.
+#
+# With --reg-cache each lifted function keeps the registers it touches in C
+# locals (_rN), which no store can alias, and keeps ctx->gpr exact wherever
+# anything else can look at it:
+#   * entry: each entry loads the registers live at its label (a region does it
+#     in that entry's switch case: regions are entered at every call and hop);
+#   * write-back: before every exit (return, SPU_RET, trampoline hand-off, the
+#     hops' and back-edge safepoints' exits), every runtime call (stop, halt,
+#     the stop service), every channel op (rdch/wrch/rchcnt) and every nested
+#     lifted call (brsl/bisl), the registers possibly written since the last
+#     sync are stored back -- a forward "may be dirty" dataflow over the
+#     function's statements (a write-back at the start of a statement leaves
+#     only that statement's own results dirty);
+#   * reload: after a nested call -- which may change any register,
+#     ctx->pic_bias or the image -- and after the channel op that runs the DMA
+#     engine (wrch MFC_Cmd: its hooks may change registers or the image), the
+#     registers live afterwards are reloaded, with ctx->ls, the LS gate and the
+#     load bias;
+#   * r0 (the link register) is written through at every def: the LS-watch
+#     diagnostic and job-verify read ctx->gpr[0] as the lr at any access/op.
+#   * "live" includes a register a later write-back stores while it is clean
+#     on some path (its local must hold the value there). A register live after
+#     a call only for that reason is instead written through at its defs
+#     (store right after the assignment, as the plain lift does) when those are
+#     plain assignments outside call-free loops: it is then never dirty, never
+#     written back and never reloaded for it.
+# Between those sync points ctx->gpr holds the values of the last sync (r0
+# aside): diagnostics that read other registers mid-function see those --
+# SPU_LS_LOWREAD's r1..r4 (an LS-access probe) and the STALL_DUMP snapshot of
+# r1/r3 (spu_coh_dump_contexts, racy from another thread anyway).
+# Halts and stops longjmp out, so they are preceded by the write-back; nothing
+# is written back after a clobbering call in the same statement. Interrupts are
+# taken only in the drain loop (SPU_DRAIN / spu_drain_call), i.e. after an exit
+# or inside a nested call: a handler sees ctx->gpr exact and what it changes is
+# loaded at the next entry or after the call. The other channel ops write no
+# register (runtime/spu: the only ctx->gpr write on a channel path is the
+# YDKJ_CRI_CHAIN r4 injection in the DMA path, behind MFC_Cmd); a runtime hook
+# that changes that must add its channel to _RC_CHAN_RELOAD. The emitted code
+# is checked against a closed list of statement forms; anything else keeps the
+# plain ctx->gpr lift of that function. Exits with the same write-back set
+# share one exit block per function; the safepoint exit of a back-edge is
+# marked unlikely, out of the loop body.
+#
+# The locals are of the vector type spu_reg (spu_helpers.h), converted to and
+# from u128 at each helper (a memcpy, no instruction). Local-store accesses use
+# spu_ls_rd/spu_ls_wr (spu_context.h): ctx->ls and ONE diagnostic gate
+# (spu_ls_gate: the LS probes, the LS watch, the SMC watch and the
+# policy-module probes folded together) are read at each entry and reload, not
+# per access; with the gate set, every access takes the full
+# spu_ls_read128/write128 path out of line. With registers in SSA form clang
+# sees il/ilhu/iohl constants right into the float helpers; their empty-asm
+# barriers (SPU__OPAQUE, SPU__RZ_OPAQUE) keep those from being folded with
+# IEEE assumptions.
+# SPU_LIFT_RCACHE_LOG=<file> lists the functions kept on ctx->gpr and why.
+#
+# Constant shufb controls (_rc_shufb_consts): a control that SPU code loads from
+# its own image (lqr, or lqa of an address inside a non-PIC image -- the
+# constant pool: splat and word-interleave patterns), possibly combined with
+# immediates, is opaque to the compiler, so every shufb costs two pshufb, a
+# blend and the special-selector OR. A forward constant propagation over the
+# function's statements (entries, nested calls and channel reads give unknown
+# values) finds the value each control register would have if the image still
+# holds its lift-time bytes there; such a shufb becomes spu_shufb_k with that
+# value, which uses the literal only after comparing it with the register
+# (spu_helpers.h). The bytes are a guess, never an assumption: whatever local
+# store holds at run time, the result is the shufb of the register's value.
+# SPU_LIFT_SHUFK=0 turns it off.
+#
+# Speculative windows (_rc_windows): a long straight run of side-effect-free
+# statements inside a loop is emitted twice -- a fast copy with the fast-path
+# tests of its float helpers deferred to one test at its end, and the
+# statements themselves, which run only when that test fails (spu_helpers.h,
+# "speculative windows", has the forms and why the result is always the
+# statements' own). SPU_LIFT_WINDOWS=0 turns it off; SPU_LIFT_WIN_MIN=<n>
+# (default 4) is the fewest float ops a window must hold, SPU_LIFT_WIN_BIG=<n>
+# (default 64) the float ops from which a run keeps its estimate ops inside.
+
